book.chapter(42, "Reference, glossary, and publishing the book")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A compact language map"
.pp
Definitions use :=, separate signatures use ::, and inline val annotations
use a single colon. Functions use named formals or numbered tacit references;
sections bind numbered hash holes locally. Application is usually written
with whitespace. A value pipeline uses |>, forward composition uses >>,
and backward composition uses <<. Types guide the supported interpretation
of juxtaposition, so explicit composition remains useful when readability
would otherwise depend on an obscure inference decision.
.lua
book.grammar("Selected surface forms", {
    {"definition", 'name parameters* ":=" expression'},
    {"signature", 'name "::" type'},
    {"lambda", '"fn" parameters+ "->" expression'},
    {"local", '"let" ["rec"] binding "in" expression'},
    {"match", '"match" expression "with" branches'},
    {"structure", '"module" Name ":=" "struct" items "end"'},
    {"sealed", '"module" Name ":>" Signature ":=" structure_body'}
})
.endlua
.pp
This is a reading aid, not a replacement for the parser. Parenthesization,
operator precedence, layout boundaries, pattern syntax, and module paths
require the fuller explanations in the language chapters. Keep separate
definitions at column one and indent continuations. Ordinary evaluation is
eager; modules initialize lazily. Strings count bytes unless a specifically
UTF-8-aware operation says otherwise. Integer operations wrap unless a
checked API returns failure instead.
.sh 2 "Terminology worth keeping precise"
.pp
A dialect is one host implementation, C++ or D. A binding associates a name
with a value-producing definition. A closure pairs code with a captured
environment. A section is an expression elaborated into a function through
local holes. A row is the extensible portion of a record type. A signature
describes a module interface; ascription checks it; opaque sealing hides
representation details and restricts visibility. A functor maps a module
argument to a module result.
.pp
An AST records abstract syntax; a concrete syntax representation may also
retain trivia needed for editing. A visitor interprets or transforms nodes
through systematic dispatch. A rewrite replaces a matching structure.
Normalization seeks a state with no applicable rule. Equality saturation
retains equivalent alternatives until no further change or a limit occurs.
Extraction selects a representative. These operations are related but
should not be used as interchangeable names for "optimizing a tree."
.sh 2 "Find authoritative contracts"
.pp
MetaML.hpp and MetaML.d contain the language implementations. Portable module
signatures live in metalib directories containing mod.ml. Host installers
and implementations live under metalib/cpp and metalib/d. The genuine C ABI
is metalib/c/MetaML.h. DSLtk.hpp and DSLtk.d define the toolkit; native
examples and tests show its supported interfaces. The compact metaml-manual
provides additional signature lookup alongside this book's longer explanations.
.pp
Prefer implementation and executable fixtures when an older design sketch
describes behavior that has changed. In particular, do not infer lazy
ordinary evaluation, C++17 compatibility, automatic prelude loading in every
native session, or implementation of every named platform service from an
old overview. Record the source revision used when documenting behavior.
A useful reference tells readers how to verify a claim, not merely where
the claim was first written.
.sh 2 "The publication pipeline"
.pp
This book is authored in the me macro language with embedded Lua blocks.
Build.sh invokes the bundled luaROFF preprocessor, whose executable is pplua,
then groff with pic, tbl, and eqn support. Custom rofflets under
luaROFF/rofflets implement code highlighting, graphs, pseudocode, grammars,
equations, notes, symbols, and UML-style diagrams. Graphs are native vector
drawings in PDF, with readable textual counterparts in the text edition.
.lua
book.code("sh", [=[metaml-book/build.sh --build-luaroff --format all --check
metaml-book/build.sh --no-build-luaroff --format pdf
metaml-book/build.sh --pplua /opt/bin/pplua --format txt
metaml-book/build.sh --install-luaroff --prefix "$HOME/.local" --format pdf]=],
    "Build locally, reuse a binary, or install luaROFF explicitly")
.endlua
.pp
Auto mode uses an explicitly selected executable, an existing local build,
or an installed pplua before compiling the bundled source if necessary.
Installation is optional and never requires the book renderer to use the
installed copy: the local build remains usable, including with staged
installation through DESTDIR. LuaROFF compilation requires CMake, C++17,
Lua 5.4 headers and libraries, and bundled sol2; the MetaML interpreter
checked by the book separately requires C++20.
.sh 2 "Authoring and checking"
.pp
Each chapter declares its number and exact title through book.chapter.
The shared Lua driver verifies order and completeness. Code listings use
book.code with an explicit language and a Lua long string. Complete MetaML
listings contain assertions and run in fresh processes. Expected-error
listings name a diagnostic code. Native fragments and pseudocode are labelled
so readers do not mistake illustrative structure for a complete executable.
.pp
The structural checker inventories all 42 chapters, validates substantial
prose and required sections, and records source hashes in a manifest.
Executable checking runs the MetaML listings and rofflet smoke tests.
The renderer treats preprocessor and groff diagnostics as build failures
and publishes artifacts only after successful rendering. These checks do
not replace editorial review, but they prevent missing chapters, broken
examples, and silently failed Lua generation from masquerading as a
finished volume.
.sh 2 "Maintain examples as interfaces evolve"
.pp
When a public API changes, update the explanation, successful examples,
failure cases, and any affected source references together. Keep standalone
listings independent rather than relying on definitions hidden in earlier
chapters. Use deterministic fixtures and avoid interactive prompts in code
intended for batch execution. Regenerate both PDF and text so improvements
to diagrams or notation remain accessible in both editions.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read metaml-book/README.md, build.sh, macros/book.lua, scripts/check_book.py,
and luaROFF/rofflets/README.md for the publishing implementation. Extend one
chapter with a new checked example and a diagram, then rebuild every output
format. As a final project, extend chapter 31 into a named-expression
language with source-aware errors and a sealed public interface. The book's
central method remains the same: establish explicit representations,
preserve their invariants, and test the boundaries between them.
